This version stores the dynamic programming values directly inside `coins`. When processing index `i`, all positions to the right already contain their minimum future costs. The smallest value in the allowed next range is added to the current price.

For `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, the last value stays `4`. At index `1`, the best future value is `4`, so its stored value becomes `6`. At index `0`, the best of the next positions is `4`, giving `5`.

The input array is changed by this code, so a copy is needed if the original prices must be kept.

```java
class Solution {
    public int MinimumCoins(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int n = coins.length;

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int last = Math.min(n, 2 * i + 2);
            int bestFuture = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

            for (int next = i + 1; next <= last; next++) {
                bestFuture = Math.min(bestFuture, coins[next]);
            }

            coins[i] += bestFuture;
        }

        return n == 0 ? 0 : coins[0];
    }
}
```

The extra array is avoided, but the input mutation is the trade-off. The nested scan takes `O(n^2)` time, and the algorithm uses `O(1)` extra space apart from the input array.

The useful part here is the range of the next item we may pay for. After paying for index `i`, the next paid index can be from `i + 1` through `2 * i + 2`, limited by the array length.

A memoized function keeps the answer for each starting index. The first item cannot be skipped, while later items are handled by the same rule.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] memo;
    private int[] prices;
    private int n;

    public int MinimumCoins(int[] coins, int amount) {
        prices = coins;
        n = coins.length;
        memo = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(memo, -1);
        return solve(0);
    }

    private int solve(int index) {
        if (index >= n) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (memo[index] != -1) {
            return memo[index];
        }

        int last = Math.min(n, 2 * index + 2);
        int nextCost = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int next = index + 1; next <= last; next++) {
            nextCost = Math.min(nextCost, solve(next));
        }

        memo[index] = prices[index] + nextCost;
        return memo[index];
    }
}
```

A one-item input returns its only price, and an empty input returns `0`. There are `n` states, but each state checks up to `n` next positions, so the time complexity is `O(n^2)`. The memo array and recursion stack use `O(n)` space.

The DP formula only needs the minimum value in a moving interval. A deque can keep those future DP values in increasing order, so the smallest one is always at the front.

The interval for index `i` is `[i + 1, min(n, 2 * i + 2)]`. Processing from right to left lets each index enter and leave the deque once. This brings the repeated range scans down to linear time.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <deque>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    int MinimumCoins(vector<int>& coins, int amount) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(coins.size());
        (void)amount;

        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);
        deque<int> window;

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int last = min(n, 2 * i + 2);

            while (!window.empty() && window.back() > last) {
                window.pop_back();
            }

            int newIndex = i + 1;
            while (!window.empty() && dp[window.back()] >= dp[newIndex]) {
                window.pop_back();
            }
            window.push_back(newIndex);

            dp[i] = coins[i] + dp[window.front()];
        }

        return dp[0];
    }
};
```

The array is not modified. The deque stores only valid future indices, and its front gives the cheapest continuation. Each index is pushed and removed at most once, giving `O(n)` time and `O(n)` extra space.

Instead of storing the farthest free position, this keeps the number of free items still available. Buying index `i` gives `i + 1` free positions after it. Skipping one item uses one of those free positions.

The two choices are simple: buy the current item, or skip it when `free_count` is positive. The cache prevents the same state from being calculated again.

```python
class Solution:
    def MinimumCoins(self, coins, amount):
        n = len(coins)
        _ = amount
        memo = {}

        def solve(index, free_count):
            if index == n:
                return 0

            state = (index, free_count)
            if state in memo:
                return memo[state]

            buy = coins[index] + solve(index + 1, index + 1)
            skip = float("inf")

            if free_count > 0:
                skip = solve(index + 1, free_count - 1)

            memo[state] = min(buy, skip)
            return memo[state]

        return solve(0, 0)
```

For `[5]`, buying the only item is required. If all items are covered by a previous purchase, the skip branch handles them without adding cost. There are up to `O(n^2)` states, and each state has constant work, so the time complexity is `O(n^2)`. The cache and recursion stack use `O(n^2)` space.
